Let $(M,g)$ be a time-oriented Lorentzian manifold of dimension $n\ge 2$ (a spacetime), and let $\tau_M$ denote its underlying manifold topology (the topology of $M$ as a smooth manifold). Recall that many alternative topologies on $M$ have been proposed in the literature, including the Alexandrov topology generated by chronological diamonds $I^+(p)\cap I^-(q)$, the Zeeman fine topology, the path topology, and various order-induced topologies derived from the causal structure $\le$ or chronological relation $\ll$. Recall also that a foliation of $M$ is a decomposition of $M$ into a disjoint union of immersed submanifolds (leaves) of fixed codimension, and that a spacelike foliation refers to one whose leaves are spacelike hypersurfaces (Cauchy surfaces in the globally hyperbolic case).

Problem. Construct, or prove the nonexistence of, a topology $\tau$ on $M$ with the following properties:
(i) $\tau \neq \tau_M$;
(ii) $\tau$ is "physically meaningful" in the sense that homeomorphisms of $(M,\tau)$ onto itself coincide (up to a specified group) with causal automorphisms or isometries of $(M,g)$, or in the sense that $\tau$ encodes the causal/chronological structure of $(M,g)$;
(iii) $\tau$ is "mathematically fruitful" in the sense that $(M,\tau)$ supports a useful theory of convergence, compactness, or continuity adapted to relativistic problems (for instance, suitable for studying singularities or formulations of the strong cosmic censorship conjecture);
(iv) $\tau$ interacts with a given foliation $\mathcal{F}$ of $M$ in a prescribed way, e.g. the leaves of $\mathcal{F}$ are closed in $\tau$, the quotient $M/\mathcal{F}$ inherits a Hausdorff topology from $\tau$, or $\tau$ restricted to each leaf agrees with the induced Riemannian topology.

Formulate explicit criteria making (ii)–(iv) precise, and either exhibit a topology $\tau$ satisfying all of them that does not already appear in the standard list (manifold, Alexandrov, Zeeman, path, order topologies), or prove that any topology satisfying these criteria must coincide with one already known.

